// ============================================================
// FILE: server/prisma/schema.prisma
// ============================================================
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS:
//   The Prisma Schema is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the entire database.
//   You write this declarative schema, and Prisma handles everything else:
//   1. Generates TypeScript types for your models (auto-complete in controllers!)
//   2. Generates SQL migration files to create/alter your database tables
//   3. Generates the PrismaClient ORM used to query the database
//
// WORKFLOW:
//   1. Edit schema.prisma (add a field, a model, a relation)
//   2. Run: npx prisma migrate dev --name "your_change_description"
//   3. Prisma generates a SQL migration file and applies it to the DB
//   4. Prisma re-generates the PrismaClient with updated TypeScript types
//
// INTERVIEW Q: What is Prisma?
// A: Prisma is a next-generation TypeScript ORM (Object-Relational Mapper).
//    Instead of writing raw SQL, you define models in schema.prisma and
//    use Prisma's type-safe client to query the DB. It catches type errors
//    at compile time (e.g., passing a string where an Int is required).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ── GENERATOR BLOCK ────────────────────────────────────────
// Tells Prisma WHAT to generate.
// provider = "prisma-client"  → Generates the PrismaClient TypeScript library.
// output = "../generated/prisma" → WHERE to save the generated client code.
//   The generated folder contains TypeScript types for User, Project, etc.
//   You import from this folder in your code:
//     import { PrismaClient } from '../generated/prisma/client.js'
// After any schema change, run: npx prisma generate
// to re-create the generated client with updated types.
generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client"
  output   = "../generated/prisma"
}

// ── DATASOURCE BLOCK ────────────────────────────────────────
// Tells Prisma WHICH database to connect to and what dialect to use.
// provider = "postgresql" → Prisma will generate PostgreSQL-compatible SQL.
//   Other options: "mysql", "sqlite", "mongodb", "sqlserver".
//   Affects the SQL syntax in migration files (e.g., SERIAL vs AUTO_INCREMENT).
// Note: The actual connection URL (DATABASE_URL) is intentionally NOT here!
//   It is read from configs/prisma.ts via process.env.DATABASE_URL.
//   Keeping credentials out of schema.prisma prevents accidental secret leaks.
datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

// ── DATA MODELS ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Each `model` block maps to a TABLE in the PostgreSQL database.
// Each field maps to a COLUMN in that table.
// Prisma generates TypeScript types matching each model.

// ── USER MODEL ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Maps to the "User" table in PostgreSQL.
//
// id: String @id
//   PRIMARY KEY. Type is String (not Int) because the ID comes from CLERK.
//   When Clerk creates a user, it generates its own ID (e.g., "user_2abc123xyz").
//   Our webhook (clerk.ts) receives this ID and stores it here.
//   Benefit: prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id: clerkUserId } }) — no mapping needed.
//
// credits: Int @default(20)
//   Credit balance for generating images and videos.
//   Every new user starts with 20 FREE credits (applied by the DATABASE on insert).
//   Image generation costs 5 credits. Video generation costs 10 credits.
//
// createdAt: DateTime @default(now())
//   Automatically set to the current timestamp when the row is created.
//   SQL equivalent: DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
//
// updatedAt: DateTime @updatedAt
//   Automatically updated to the current timestamp whenever the row changes.
//   Useful for "last seen" logic, cache invalidation, audit trails.
//
// projects: Project[]
//   One User can have MANY Projects → one-to-many relation (the "one" side).
//   In queries: prisma.user.findUnique({ include: { projects: true } })
//   → Returns the user WITH all their projects embedded.
model User {
  id        String    @id
  email     String
  name      String
  image     String
  credits   Int       @default(20)
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  projects  Project[]
}

// ── PROJECT MODEL ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Maps to the "Project" table in PostgreSQL.
//
// id: String @id @default(uuid())
//   Random UUID (v4) generated by the database automatically.
//   WHY UUID instead of sequential Int IDs?
//   1. Security: Attackers cannot enumerate records (1, 2, 3...).
//   2. Distributed: Multiple servers can generate IDs without coordination.
//   3. Globally unique: Can merge databases without ID collisions.
//
// userId: String
//   Foreign key referencing User.id — tracks which user owns this project.
//
// productDescription: String @default("")
//   Optional product details passed to Gemini AI for context.
//   @default("") → empty string instead of null. Avoids null-check complexity.
//
// userPrompt: String @default("")
//   Optional additional instruction from the user for the AI.
//   Example: "Make it look like a beach vacation photo"
//
// aspectRatio: String @default("9:16")
//   "9:16" = vertical (TikTok/Reels), "16:9" = horizontal.
//   Passed to both Gemini image config and Veo video config.
//
// targetLength: Int @default(5)
//   Target duration in seconds for the generated video.
//
// uploadedImages: String[]
//   Array of Cloudinary URLs for the uploaded product + model images.
//   In PostgreSQL, String[] = TEXT[] (native array column type).
//   Example: ["https://res.cloudinary.com/.../product.jpg", "https://.../model.jpg"]
//
// generatedImage: String @default("")
//   Cloudinary URL of the AI-generated composite image.
//   Empty string initially. Updated after Gemini image generation completes.
//
// generatedVideo: String @default("")
//   Cloudinary URL of the AI-generated video.
//   Empty string initially. Updated after Veo video generation completes.
//
// isGenerating: Boolean @default(false)
//   TRUE while an AI job is in progress (image or video generation).
//   The frontend polls the project endpoint every 10 seconds and checks this flag.
//   When it flips to false, polling stops and the result is displayed.
//
// isPublished: Boolean @default(false)
//   TRUE when the user clicks "Publish" → project appears in the Community gallery.
//   FALSE = private, only visible in "My Generations".
//
// error: String @default("")
//   Stores error messages if the AI generation fails.
//   Allows debugging without losing the project record.
//
// user: User @relation(...)
//   Declares the Many-to-One relation back to User.
//   fields: [userId]  → the foreign key ON THIS TABLE is `userId`
//   references: [id]  → references the `id` column on the User table
//   onDelete: Cascade → if the User is deleted, all their Projects are deleted too.
//     Without this, deleting a user would fail (projects still reference them).
//     This is REFERENTIAL INTEGRITY — the DB ensures no orphaned rows.
//
// INTERVIEW Q: What is a CASCADE delete?
// A: A database constraint that automatically deletes related rows when the
//    parent row is deleted. Prevents orphaned data (projects without a user).
//    Alternatives: SET NULL (set userId to NULL) or RESTRICT (block the delete).
model Project {
  id                 String   @id @default(uuid())
  name               String
  userId             String
  productName        String
  productDescription String   @default("")
  userPrompt         String   @default("")
  aspectRatio        String   @default("9:16")
  targetLength       Int      @default(5)
  uploadedImages     String[]
  generatedImage     String   @default("")
  generatedVideo     String   @default("")
  isGenerating       Boolean  @default(false)
  isPublished        Boolean  @default(false)
  error              String   @default("")
  createdAt          DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt          DateTime @updatedAt
  user               User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
}

/*
  ============================================================
  CONCEPT BOXES
  ============================================================

  CONCEPT: ORM vs Raw SQL
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Raw SQL:
    db.query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1", [userId])
    → No type safety. Typos in column names crash at runtime.
    → SQL injection risk if not parameterized correctly.
    → Verbose for complex queries.

  Prisma ORM:
    prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id: userId } })
    → TypeScript knows `id` is a String. Type error if you pass a number.
    → No SQL injection risk (Prisma parameterizes all queries).
    → Readable, self-documenting syntax.
    → Auto-generates types → full IDE autocomplete.

  CONCEPT: Database Migrations
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A migration = a versioned file describing a DB schema change.

  Example: Adding `credits` column to User table.
  Migration file (SQL):
    ALTER TABLE "User" ADD COLUMN "credits" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 20;

  Why migrations matter:
  1. Version control for your database schema.
  2. Reproducible: Any developer can rebuild the exact DB from migration history.
  3. Safe rollbacks: If a migration breaks production, you can roll it back.
  4. Team collaboration: Merge migration files like code files.

  ============================================================
  MEMORY NOTES
  ============================================================
  ✔ schema.prisma = single source of truth for database structure
  ✔ User.id = String (from Clerk) — not auto-increment Int
  ✔ User.credits @default(20) = free credits for every new signup
  ✔ Project.id @default(uuid()) = random UUID for security
  ✔ Project.uploadedImages String[] = PostgreSQL array column
  ✔ Project.isGenerating = polled by frontend every 10s
  ✔ onDelete: Cascade = deleting user deletes all their projects
  ✔ @updatedAt = auto-updated timestamp on every row change
  ✔ generator output "../generated/prisma" = where PrismaClient is generated

  IF THIS FILE IS REMOVED:
  ✘ The entire database structure is lost
  ✘ npx prisma migrate dev will have nothing to work from
  ✘ The TypeScript client cannot be regenerated
*/
